simplybs: fall back to the GNU origin, not just the redirector.

Every GNU package in simplybs points at ftpmirror.gnu.org, which is a
redirector that load-balances to a random GNU mirror. It has host-wide
outages -- every path returns 502, not just one file -- and when that
happens the whole dependency graph is unbuildable, since binutils, m4,
make, tar, gdb, gettext and two dozen others all resolve through it.
Patch 0004 keeps the origin retrying instead of giving up on the first
empty response, but fifteen retries against a host that is down is
still a failed build.

Rewrite the host to ftp.gnu.org, the origin the redirector points at,
for the origin attempt only. Doing it here rather than in the package
JSONs is deliberate: the mirror key is URLToPath() of the URL as
written in the JSON, so leaving that spelling alone keeps
static.mrcyjanek.net usable for the packages it actually carries, and
changes only what happens once the mirrors have missed.

The path has to be adjusted as well as the host. ftpmirror.gnu.org/<pkg>/
resolves to <mirror>/gnu/<pkg>/, so the "gnu/" component belongs in the
rewritten URL. 39 of the 41 ftpmirror entries under packages/ are already
written .../gnu/... and need nothing; native/bash and native/cpio are not,
and a bare host swap sends them to ftp.gnu.org/bash/ and ftp.gnu.org/cpio/,
which are 404 (the /gnu/ spellings are 200). Those two are not obscure:
native/bash is a dependency of unbound, which monero_c builds for every
target, and native/cpio is one of native/apple-sdk, so of all four Apple
targets. Getting it wrong turns the outage this patch exists for into
sixteen guaranteed-404 retries for exactly those packages.

Both URL schemes have to be matched. Of the 41 ftpmirror.gnu.org
entries under packages/, 36 are written http:// and only 5 https://,
so matching https:// alone leaves native/ct-tools (gdb) and most of
the rest still pointed at the redirector.

This cannot affect what gets built. Each download entry pins a sha256
that downloadWithResume() checks, so a file from the wrong host fails
the same way a corrupted one does. Verified for the case that hit us:
binutils-2.45.tar.bz2 from ftp.gnu.org is byte-identical to the pinned
1393f90db70c2ebd785fb434d6127f8888c559d5eeb9c006c354b203bab3473e.

diff --git a/simplybs/utils/download/download.go b/simplybs/utils/download/download.go
index 16ef92d..b700275 100644
--- a/simplybs/utils/download/download.go
+++ b/simplybs/utils/download/download.go
@@ -117,6 +117,38 @@ func DownloadFile(packageName, path, url, expectedSha256 string, isMirror bool)
 		}
 	}
 
+	// ftpmirror.gnu.org is GNU's redirector, which load-balances to a random
+	// mirror and has host-wide outages -- every GNU path 502s, not just one
+	// file -- stranding every GNU package in the graph. ftp.gnu.org is the
+	// origin it redirects to, and stays up when the redirector does not.
+	//
+	// Both schemes have to be handled: 36 of the 41 GNU entries in packages/
+	// are spelled http://, only 5 https://.
+	//
+	// This rewrites only the origin attempt, deliberately after the mirror loop
+	// above: the mirror key comes from URLToPath() on the URL as written in the
+	// package JSON, so leaving that spelling alone keeps the LFS mirror usable
+	// for the packages it carries. Integrity is unaffected either way -- every
+	// download entry pins a sha256 that downloadWithResume() verifies, so the
+	// host a file arrives from cannot change what gets built.
+	if !isMirror {
+		for _, redirector := range []string{"http://ftpmirror.gnu.org/", "https://ftpmirror.gnu.org/"} {
+			if strings.HasPrefix(url, redirector) {
+				rest := strings.TrimPrefix(url, redirector)
+				// The redirector maps <pkg>/... onto <mirror>/gnu/<pkg>/..., so
+				// the path component has to be put back for the origin. 39 of
+				// the 41 entries in packages/ already spell it; native/bash and
+				// native/cpio do not, and ftp.gnu.org/bash/ is a 404.
+				if !strings.HasPrefix(rest, "gnu/") {
+					rest = "gnu/" + rest
+				}
+				url = "https://ftp.gnu.org/" + rest
+				log.Printf("Using GNU origin instead of the redirector: %s", url)
+				break
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(path), 0755)
 
 	maxRetries := 15
